  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1225 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nBroad southwesterly flow aloft is expected across the Great Plains \nWednesday and Thursday as a pair of troughs will be present across \nthe Western US, one in the polar jetstream and the other embedded in \nthe subtropical jetstream (STJ). A weak low pressure system is \nfavored to develop in northeastern New Mexico/southeastern Colorado \nwhich will aid in creating breezy conditions across the Panhandles. \nExpecting highs in the 70s to low-80s on Wednesday and Thursday with \ndry conditions.\n\nA shortwave embedded in the STJ is favored to track into \nnorthwestern Mexico and move northeast into west Texas Thursday \nnight and into Friday. Current guidance suggests the trough axis may \nstay off to our south and east, but there would still be plenty of \nmoisture and forcing to initiate shower and thunderstorm activity \nacross much of the Panhandles. In a 24-hour period ending 7 PM \nFriday, there is a 20-50% chance in the southeastern half of the \nTexas Panhandle for rain totals to exceed 0.50\", and 10-25% chance \nto exceed 1\" for the same area. \n\nExpecting near-normal temperatures across the Panhandles this \nweekend as one trough moves out and another approaches from the \nwest. Could see rain showers develop Saturday afternoon, but that \ndepends on the evolution/placement of the subtropical jet and timing \nof embedded disturbances. \n\nThe next weather system of interest is currently expected to begin \nimpacting the Panhandles late Sunday but especially next Monday.\n\nVanden Bosch",
